      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand and isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(x \\ln 7\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8 - x \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the right side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\ln 7 = x (\\ln 8 - \\ln 7)\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 8 - \\ln 7\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite \\(x\\) as:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nBy comparing this with the expression we found for \\(x\\), we see that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7}\n\\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln 8 - \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can simplify the right side:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(b = \\frac{8}{7}\\).\n\nThe value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\\).",
